# CapabilityBinding — the assistant-owned CRD that carries capability
# documents, replacing the per-turn pull from a catalog adapter.
#
# GENERATED from pkg/apis/capabilities/v1alpha1 by controller-gen; the Go types
# are the source of truth. Regenerate with:
#   controller-gen crd paths=./pkg/apis/capabilities/v1alpha1/... \
#     output:crd:dir=config/crd
#
# Why a CRD and not the aggregated apiserver: capability config is WRITTEN by
# providers and READ by the assistant, which is the opposite direction from
# conversations. etcd-backed storage, admission validation, and a watch come
# free; the aggregated server would have had to grow all three.
#
# Why this group is not assistant.miloapis.com: config/components/api-registration
# registers APIService v1alpha1.assistant.miloapis.com, so the kube-aggregator
# owns that whole group/version. A CRD registered there would be admitted and
# then permanently shadowed — writes appear to succeed, reads route to the
# aggregated server, which has never heard of the kind.
#
# The schema is a field-for-field mirror of capability.CapabilitySpec
# (internal/capability/document.go). Moving the required-field checks here means
# a malformed document is rejected at kubectl apply, by the producer, instead of
# being silently skipped hours later inside a customer's turn.
#
# Why scope: Cluster. A Milo project is a virtual control plane over one
# apiserver, partitioned by an etcd key prefix, and it has its own namespace set
# (milo-system, default) with nothing named after the project. The plane IS the
# tenancy boundary, so an object inside it needs no namespace — the same reason
# the service catalog's AgentBinding, which produces these, is cluster-scoped.
# Namespacing it would have meant LISTing a namespace nobody creates and every
# project composing as though it had bought nothing.
